Q: Difference between persist(), merge(), and save()?

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 1. persist() — JPA EntityManager

Purpose: make a **new transient** entity managed → scheduled INSERT.

User user = new User();      // transient
user.setName("Ali");
entityManager.persist(user); // now managed (INSERT on flush/commit)

Key points:
- For new entities
- Returns void
- Entity becomes managed in persistence context
- SQL INSERT usually happens on flush/commit (not always instantly)
- If already managed/persistent incorrectly → can throw EntityExistsException

Use when: you know it’s a new record and you use EntityManager directly.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 2. merge() — JPA EntityManager

Purpose: copy state into persistence context → returns a **managed copy**.
Common for **detached** entities (e.g. came from another request/session).

User detached = new User();
detached.setId(1L);
detached.setName("Updated Name");

User managed = entityManager.merge(detached);
// managed is managed; detached stays detached

Key points:
- Works on detached / existing state
- Returns managed entity (use the returned instance)
- Original object remains detached
- If row exists → UPDATE (on flush)
- If no matching row → can INSERT

Trap: always continue with the **returned** managed entity from merge().

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 3. save() — Spring Data JpaRepository

Convenience API. Internally roughly:

if (entityIsNew)
    em.persist(entity);
else
    em.merge(entity);

User user = new User();
user.setName("Awais");
userRepository.save(user); // persist or merge

Key points:
- Handles insert + update in one method
- Returns saved entity
- “Is new?” usually means: id == null (or @Version null / Persistable.isNew())

🔥 Big Spring interview trap:
If you manually set id on a new object:
user.setId(100L);
repo.save(user);
→ Spring may treat it as NOT new → calls **merge()** (SELECT then UPDATE/INSERT),
not persist(). Prefer generated IDs, or implement Persistable carefully.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔁 Summary

| Method      | From            | Best for              | Returns        | SQL idea        |
| ----------- | --------------- | --------------------- | -------------- | --------------- |
| persist()   | EntityManager   | new transient         | void           | INSERT          |
| merge()     | EntityManager   | detached / upserty    | managed copy   | UPDATE/INSERT   |
| save()      | Spring Data JPA | everyday Spring apps  | saved entity   | persist or merge|

Entity states reminder:
transient → no id/persistence context
managed  → tracked by persistence context
detached → had id, no longer in context
removed  → scheduled delete

────────────────────────────────────────
When to use

- persist(): EntityManager + sure it’s new
- merge(): re-attach detached DTO/entity graph
- save(): Spring Boot repositories (most common day-to-day)

Interview answer:
“persist inserts a new managed entity and returns void. merge copies a detached entity into the context and returns the managed instance.
Spring Data save chooses persist for new entities and merge for existing ones based on id/version — with the caveat that a manually assigned id can force merge.”
